---------------------------------------------------- WEB DEVELOPMENT ---------------------------------------------------------

Introduction to Web Development
 Web Development is the process of creating and maintaining websits and web pages

Types of Web development:

1.Frontend development
  Creates the user interface (UI)
  Everything the user can see and interact with

 Technologies:
 HTML
 CSS
 JavaScript

2.Backend Development
 Handles server-side logic
 Manages databases, authentication, and APIs

 Technologies:
 Node.js
 Python
 Java
 PHP
 MySQL
 MongoDB

3.Full Stack Development
 Works on both Frontend and Backend

What is a Web Browser?
 A web browser is software used to access and display websites.
Examples:
 Google Chrome
 Microsoft Edge
 Mozilla Firefox
 Safari

What is a Web Server?
A web server stores website files and sends them to users when requested.
Examples:
 Apache
 Nginx

Website:
 A collection of interconnected web pages

Webpage:
 A single page of a website

URL (Uniform Resource Locator):
 The address of a webpage

HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol):
 The protocol used for communication between the browser and the web server

HTTPS:
 A secure version of HTTP

HTML (HyperText Markup Language)
 HTML is the standard markup language used to create the structure of web pages
 HTML is not a programming languages
 It tells the browser how to display content on a webpages

What can HTML create?
 Headings
 Paragraphs
 Images
 Links
 Tables
 Lists
 Forms
 Buttons
 Videos
 Audio

HTML File Extension:
 HTML files are saved with the .html extension

Examples:
 index.html
 about.html
 contact.html
 
Basic HTML structur:
 Every HTML document follows a basic structure
Syntax:

 <!DOCTYPE html>
 <html>
 <head>
     <title> My website </title>
 </head>

 <body>
     
      hii

 </body>
 </html>

<!DOCTYPE html>
 Declares that the document is an HTML document
 It helps the browser render the webpage correctly

<html>
 The root element of an HTML document
 All HTML code is written inside this tag

<head>
 Contains information about the webpage
 This information is not displayed on the webpages

Examples:
 Title
 Meta tags
 CSS files
 JavaScript files

<title>
 Define the title of the webpage
 The title appears on the browser Tab

Example:
 <title> My website </title>

<body>
 Contains all the visible content of the webpage

Examples:
 Headings
 Paragraphs
 Images
 Links
 Tables
 Forms
 Lists

HTML tags
 HTML uses tags to define elements

 Most tags have:
 Opening tag
 <tag>
 closing tag
 </tag>

Example:
 <p>Hello World</p>

Nested tags:
 HTML tags can be placed inside other tags
Example:
    <html>
        <body>
            <h1>welcome</h1>
        </body>
    </html>

HTML tags:
 HTML provides 6 levels of heading to define titles and subtitles on a webpage

Syntax
 <h1>Heading 1</h1>   -> Largest and most important heading
 <h2>Heading 2</h2>   -> Second largest heading  
 <h3>Heading 3</h3>   -> Third largest heading
 <h4>Heading 4</h4>   -> Fourth largest heading
 <h5>Heading 5</h5>   -> Fifth largest  heading
 <h6>Heading 6</H6>   -> Smallest heading

Paragraph:
 The <p> tag is used to create a paragraph

Syntax:
 <p> paragraph </p>

Anchor Tag(Links):
 The <a> tag is used to create hyperlinks

Syntax:
 <a href="URL">Link Name</a>
 
href Attribute:
 The href attribute specifies the destination (URL) of the Link

Opening a Link in a New Tab:
 Use the target="_blank" attribute

Examples:
 <a href="https://www.google.com" target="_blank">Google</a>

HTML Attributes
 Attributes provide additional information about HTML elements

Syntax:
 <tag attribute="value">Content</tag>

Example:
 <a href="https://www.google.com">Google</a>

 href -> Attribute
 "https://www.google.com" -> Value

Line Break:
 The <br> tag moves the content to the next Line

Horizontal Line:
 The <hr> tag inserts a horizontal Line

HTML Comments
 Comments are not displayed on the webpages

Syntax:
 <!-- This is a comment -->

Image in HTML:
 The <img> tag is used to display an image on a webpage

Syntax:
 <img src ="image.jpg" alt="Image Description">

Attributes:
->arc(Source)
 Specifies the path of the image
  
->alt (Alternative Text)
 Displays text if the image cannot be loaded
 Improves accessibility

Examples:
 <img src="hello.jpg" alt="hello">

Image Width and Height:
 The width and height attributes are used to set the image size

Example:
 <img src="cat.jpg" a;t="cat" width="300" height="200">

Lists in HTML:
 HTML provides three types of Lists

1.Ordered List (<ol>)
  Display items in numbered Order

Example:
   <ol>
       <li>web</li>
       <li>banana</li> 
       <li>development</li>
    </ol>

2.Unordered List(<ul>)
  Display items using bullets

Example:
    <ul>
       <li>web</li>
       <li>mango</li>
       <li>development</li>
     </ul>

3.Description List(<dl>)
 Used to display terms and their descriptions

Tags Used:
 <dl> -> Description List
 <dt> -> Description term
 <dd> -> Description Definition

Example:
 <dl>
    <dt>HTML</dt>
    <dd>HyperText Markup Language</dd>

    <dt>CSS</dt>
    <dd>Cascading Style Sheets</dd>
  </dl>

Tables in HTML:
 The <Table> tag is used to create Tables

Structure:
  <table>
       <tr>
           <th>Name</th>
           <th>Age</th>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Abhishek</td>
            <td>20</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

Table Tags:

<Table> -> Define a table

<tr> -> Defines a table row

<th> -> Defines a table heading

<td> -> Defines table data (cell)

Colspan Attribute
 The colspan attribute allows a cell to span multiple columns

Example:
   <td colspan="2">Student Details</td>

rowspan Attribute
 The rowspan attribute allows a cell to span multiple rowspan

Example:
    <td rowspan="2">Abhishek</td>

SEO:
 SEO stands for Search Engine Optimization

 SEO is the process of improving a website so that it can rank better in search engine results

Why is SEO important?
 Improve website visibility
 Helps search engines understand the website
 Increases organic traffic
 Helps users find relevant content

Important SEO Practices
1.Title:
 The <title> tag defines the title of a webpage

2.Meta Description 
 Meta description provides a short description of the webpage

Example:
 <meta name="description" content="This is my personal website">

3.Headings
 HTML provides heading from <h1> to <h6>

 <h1>Main Heading</h1>
 <h2>Sub Heading</h2>
 <h3>Another Heading</h3>

 Headings should be used in a proper hierarchy

4.Semantic HTML
 Semantic HTML uses meaningful tags that describe the purpose of the content

Examples:
 <header>
 <nav>
 <main>
 <section>
 <article>
 <footer>

Coree Web Vitals:
 Core web vitals are metrics used to measure  important asppects of a webpage's user experience

There are three main Core Web Vitals:
1.LCP -> Largest Contentful Paint
 Measure how quickly the largest visible content loads
 Focus -> Loading Performance

2.INP ->Interaction to Next Paint
 Measures how quickly a webpage responds to user Interaction
 Focus -> Responsiveness

3.CLS -> Cumulative Layout Shift
 Meaasures unexpected movement of elements on a webpage while Loading 
 Focus -> Visual Stability

HTML Forms
 AA form  is used to collect input and information from users

Examples:
 Login Form
 Refistration Form
 Search Form
 Feedback Form

 The <form> tag is used to create a form

Basic Syntax:
 <form action="backend.php">

 </form> -> Action specifies where the form data should be sent for processing

Input Tag:
 The <input>  tag is used to create interactive input fields in a form

Syntax:
 <input type="text">

The type attribute specifies the type of input

Common Input Type:
 text
 email
 password
 number
 date 
 radio
 checkbox
 submit

Examples:
 <input type="text">
 <input type="email">
 <input type="password">
 <input type="number">
 <input type="date">

Name Attribute:
 The name attribute is used to identify the input field when the form data is submitted

Example:
 <input type="text" name="username">

GET:
 Sends data through the URL
 Data can be visible in the URL
 Suitable for non-sensitive data

Example:
 <form action="backend.php" method="get">

POST:
 Sends data in the request body
 Data is not displayed in the URL
 Commonly used for submitting sensitive or large amounts of data

Example:
 <form action="backend.php" method="post">

Label Tag:
 The <label> tag defines a label for an input field

Example:
 <label for="name">Name:</label>
 <input type="text" id="name" name="name">

 The for attribute of <label> should match the id of the input

Textarea:
 The <textarea> tag is used to create a multi-line text input field

Example:
 <textarea name="message" rows="5" cols="30">
 </textarea>

it is usedful for:
 Comments
 Feedback
 Messages
 Addresses

Submit Buttom
 The submit input is used to submit the form

Example:
 <input type="submit" value="Submit">

Example:
 <form action="backend.php" method="post">
    
     <label for="name">Name:</label>
     <input type="text" id="name" name="name">

     <br><br>

     <label for="email">Email:</label>
     <input type="email" id="email" name="email">

     <br><br>

     label for="message">Message:</label>
     <textarea id="message" name="message"></textarea>

     <br><br>

     <input type="submit" value="Submit">

 </form>

INLINE AND BLOCK ELEMENTS IN HTML:
Block-Level Elements
 A block-level elements always starts on a new line and takes up the full available width
 Take full availabble width

Inline Elements:
 An inline element does not start on a new line
 It takes only as much width as necessary

Block Elements (Most Commonly Used First)
 <div>: A generic container for flow content.
 <p>: Paragraph.
 <h1>, <h2>, <h3>, <h4>, <h5>, <h6>: Headings.
 <ul>: Unordered list.
 <ol>: Ordered list.
 <li>: List item.
 <form>: A section containing form controls.
 <table>: Table.
 <section>: A standalone section of a document.
 <header>: A container for introductory content or a set of navigational links.
 <footer>: Footer of a section or page.
 <nav>: A section of a page that contains navigation links.
 <article>: A self-contained composition in a document.
 <aside>: A section of a page that contains information indirectly related to the main content.
 <main>: The main content of a document.
 <fieldset>: A set of form controls grouped under a common name.
 <blockquote>: A block of text that is a quotation from another source.
 <pre>: Preformatted text.
 <canvas>: A container used to draw graphics via JavaScript.
 <dl>: Description list.
 <dt>: Term in a description list.
 <dd>: Description in a description list.
 <figure>: Any content that is referenced from the main content.
 <figcaption>: A caption for a <figure> element.
 <address>: Contact information for the author or owner of the document.
 <hr>: A thematic break or a horizontal rule.
 <tfoot>: Footer of a table.

Inline Elements (Most Commonly Used First)
 <a>: Anchor or hyperlink.
 <img>: Image.
 <span>: Generic inline container.
 <input>: Input field.
 <label>: Label for a form element.
 <strong>: Strong emphasis.
 <em>: Emphasized text.
 <br>: Line break.
 <code>: Code snippet.
 <b>: Bold text.
 <i>: Italic text.
 <u>: Underlined text.
 <small>: Smaller text.
 <sub>: Subscript.
 <sup>: Superscript.
 <mark>: Marked or highlighted text.
 <q>: Short inline quotation.
 <cite>: Citation.
 <kbd>: Keyboard input.
 <samp>: Sample output.
 <var>: Variable in a mathematical expression or programming context.
 <time>: Time.
 <abbr>: Abbreviation.
 <data>: Machine-readable translation of content.
 <acronym>: Acronym (Not supported in HTML5). 

IDS and Classes in HTML:

ID: An ID is a unique identifier used to identify one specific HTML element

Syntax:
 <p id="intro">Hello World</p>
 Here,intro is the ID of the paragraph

-> An id should be unique within a webpage
-> The same id should not be used for multiple Elements
-> An ID is useful when we want to identify a specific elements

Class: A class is used to group multiple HTML elements that share the same characteristics or purpose

Syntax:
 <p class="red">Hello</p>
 <p class="red">Welcome</p>
 Both paragraphs belong to the same class

-> The same class can be used on multiple elements
-> An element can have multiple Classes

Example:
 <p class="red bold">Hello</p>
 Here the paragraph has two classes:
 red
 bold

Using ID and Class Together:
 An HTML element can have both an ID and a class

 <p id="first" class="text">
     Hello World
 </p>

Here:
 id -> first
 classs -> text

Multiple Classes:
 An element can have more than one class
 <p class="text red bold">
    Hello World
 </p>
 The classes are seeperated by spaces
 text
 red
 bold

Why are IDs and Classes Used:
 IDs and classes are mainly used to:
 Identify HTML elements
 Group HTML elements
 Apply CSS styles
 Select elements using JavaScript

Example:
 <h1 id="title" class="heading">
     My website
 </h1>

 id -> uniquely identifies the heading
 class -> identifies the group/category of the element

Video Audio and Media in HTML:
HTML  allows us to add different types of media to a webpage, such as:
 Images
 Audio
 Video
 Embedded Content

1.Video in HTML
 The <video> tag is used to add a video to a webpage

Syntax:
 <video src="video.mp4" controls></video>

controls
 The controls attribute displays video controls such as:
 Play/Pause
 Volume
 FullSCREEN
 progress bar 

2.Video Attributes 
 Some commonly used attributes of the <video> tag are:

controls
 Display video controls
 <video src="video.mp4" controls></video>

autoplay
 automatically starts the video
 <video src="video.mp4" autoplay></video>

loop
 Plays the video repeatedly
 <video src="video.mp4" loop></video>

muted
 Starts the video without sound
 <video src="video.mp4" muted></video>

poster
 Displays an image before the video starts
 <video src="video.mp4" poster="thumbnail.jpg controls></video>

3.Width and Height
 We can specify the size of a video using width and height

Syntax:
 <video src="video.mp4"
        width="500"
        height="300"
        controls></video>
        
4.Audio in HTML
 The <audio> tag is used to add audio to a webpage

syntax:
 <audio src="song.mp3" controls></audio>
 The controls attribute provides audio controls such as:
 play/Pause
 Volume
 progress bar

5.Audio Attributes
controls
 Display src="song.mp3" controls></audio>

autoplay
 Automatically starts the audio
 <audio src="song.mp3" autoplay></audio>

loop 
 Repeats the audio continuously
 <audio src="song.mp3" loop></audio>

muted
 Starts the audio without sound
 <audio src="song.mp3" muted></audio>

6.Souce Tag
 The <souce> tag is used to specify media files inside <video> or <audio>
 
Example:
  <video controls>
         <source src="video.mp4" type="video/mp4">
  </video>

Audio:
 <audio controls>
        <source src="song.mp3" type="audio/mpeg">
 </audio>
 using <source> can allow us to provide different media formats

7.Iframe
 The <iframe> tag is used to embed another webpage or external content inside our webpage

Example:
 <iframe src="https://example.com"></iframe>
 youtube Videos
 Maps
 Other webpages

Semantic Tags in HTML:
 Semantic tags are HTML tags that clearly describe the meaning and purpose of their content

They make the structure of a webpage easier to understand for:
 Developers
 Browsers
 Search engines
 Screen readers

Example:
 <header>
      <h1>My Website</h1>
 </header>
 Here, <header> clearly tells us that the content is the header section of the Webpage

Why use Semantic Tags?
 Makes HTML cade easier to read and unserstand
 Gives meaning to different sections of a webpage
 Improves accessibility
 Helps search engines understand the webpage structure
 Makes website structure more organized

Important Semantic Tags:
1.<header>
 Represents the introductory/header section of a webpage or section

Example:
 <header>
      <h1>My Website</h1>
 </header>

2.<nav>
 Represents navigation links

Example:
 <nav>
    <a href="index.html">Home</a>
    <a href="about.html">About</a>
    <a href="contact.html">Contact</a>
 </nav>


3.<main>
 Represents the main content of the webpage

Example:
 <main>
    <h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
    <p>This is the main content.</p>
 </main>
 A page should generally have one main <main> element

4.<section>
 Represent a separate section of related content

Example:
 <section>
    <h2>About Me</h2>
    <p>I am a web developer.</p>
 </section>

5.<article>
 Represents independent, self-Contained content

Example:
 Blog post
 News article
 Forum post
 Product/article content

Example:
 <article>
    <h2>My First Blog</h2>
    <p>This is my first blog post</p>
 </article>

6.<aside>
 Represents content related to the main content to the main content but not part of the main flow

Examples:
 Sidebar
 Related links
 Additional information

Example:
 <aside>
    <h3>Related Links</h3>
    <a href='#'>HTML Tutorial</a>
 </aside>

7.<footer>
 Represents  the footer section of a webpage or section
 Usually contains:
 Copyright information
 Contact information
 Links
 Other additional information

Example:
 <footer>
   <p>Copyright 2026 My website</p>
 </footer>

Semantic Structure:
 A webpage can be organized like this

 +-------------------------+
 |         HEADER          |
 +-------------------------+
 |          NAV            |
 +-------------------------+
 |                         |
 |          MAIN           |
 |   +-----------------+   |
 |   |    SECTION      |   |
 |   +-----------------+   |
 |                         |
 |   +-----------------+   |
 |   |    ARTICLE      |   |
 |   +-----------------+   |
 |                         |
 +-------------------------+
 |         ASIDE           |
 +-------------------------+
 |         FOOTER          |
 +-------------------------+

Non-Semantic Tags
 Do not clearly describe the meaning of their content

Example:
 <div>
 <span>

Entities and Code Tag in HTML
1.HTML Entities
 HTML entities are special codes used to display reserved characters and special symbols in HTML
  Some characters have a special meaning in HTML.

Example:
 <p>5 < 10</p>
 The < character can be interpreted as the beginning of an HTML tag.
 So we use an entity:
 <p>5 &lt; 10</p>
 Output:
 5 < 10

Common HTML Entities
 Entity Character	  Meaning
 &lt;	   <	      Less than
 &gt;	   >	      Greater than
 &amp;	   &	      Ampersand
 &quot;	   "	      Double quotation mark
 &apos;	   '	      Apostrophe
 &copy;	   ©	      Copyright
 &nbsp;	 Space	      Non-breaking space

2.Non-Breaking Space
 &nbsp; represents a non-breaking space
 Normally, multiple spaces written in HTML are collapsed into a single visible space

Example:
 <p>Hello   world</p>
 The browser does not preserve all those spaces
Using:
 <p>Hello%nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;world</p>
 creates visible spacing between the words

3.HTML Quotation Tags
 HTML provides tags for displaying quotations

<blockquote>
 Used for a longer quotation from another source
Example:
 <blockquote>
     Learning never stops
 </blockquote>

<q>
 Used for a short inline quotation
Example:
 <p> He said <q> Hello World</q>.</p>

4.<code> Tag
 The <code> tag is used to represent a piece of computer code
Example:
 <p> Use <code>cout</code> to print output in c++.</p>
 it is useful when displaying programming code on a webpage

5.<pre> Tag
 The <pre> tag displays preformatted text
 It preserves:
 Spaces
 Line breaks
 Formatting
Example:
 <pre>
 Hello
   World
     HTML
 </pre>
 The spacing and line breaks are preserved
 
6.<code> with <pre>
 For displaying multiple lines of code, <code> can be used inside <pre>
Example:
 <Pre>
 <code>
 #include &It;iostream&gt;
 using namespace std;

 int main()
 {
    cout &It;&It; "Hello";
 }
 </code>
 </pre>
 This is useful for displaying code on a webpage

7.Prism.js
 Prism.js is a javaScript library used for syntax highlighting of code
 Syntax highlighting makes different parts of code appear in different styles/colors, making code easier to read
 It is commonly used when displaying programming code on websites

8.Obsolete HTML Tags
 Some older HTML tags are obsolete/deprecated and should generally not be used in modern HTML
Example include:
 <center>
 <font>
 <big>
 Modern websites generally use CSS for styling instead
 For example, instead of using:
 <center>Hello</center>
 CSS should be used to control alignment

Introduction to CSS:
 CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets
 Css  is used to style and design HTML webpages
 Styling of webpage

CSS is used to:
 Change text color
 Change background color
 Change font size
 Change fonts
 Add borders
 Add spacing
 Change element size
 Position elements
 Create attractive webpages

CSS Syntax:
 Basic CSS syntax:
 selector {
       property: value;   
 }

Example:
 h1 {
   color: blue;
 }

 Here:
 h1 -> Selector
 color -> Property
 blue -> value

Three Ways to Add CSS:
1.Inline CSS
2.Internal CSS
3.External CSS

1.Inline CSS
 CSS is written inside the HTML element using the style attribute

Example:
 <h1 style="color: red;">Hello worls</h1>

Adavantage:
 Easy to apply styling to a single element

Disadvantage:
 Not suitable for styling many elements because it makes HTML code lengthy

2.Internal CSS
 CSS is written inside the <style> tag, usually inside the <head> section

Example:
 <!DOCTYPE html>
 <html>

 <head>

   <style>
       h1 {
            color: blue;
       }

       p {
         color: green;
       }

   </style>
 </head>
 <body>
     <h1>Hello world</h1>
     <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
 </body>
 </html>
 Internal CSS can style multiple elements on the same webpage

3.External CSS
 CSS is written in a seprate .css file

Example:
HTML file
 <!DOCTYPE html>
 <html>

 <head>
  
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">

 </head>

 <body>
   <h1>Hello world</h1>
 </body>
 </html>

CSS file
 h1{
     color: blue;S
 }

Terms:
Selector -> Selects the HTML element that we want to style
Property -> Specifies what we want to change
Value -> Specifies the value of the property

CSS Selectors
 CSS Selectors are used to select HTML elements so that CSS styles can be applied to them

Example:
 p {
     color: red;
 }

Here:
 p -> Selector
 color -> Property
 red -> Value

Element Selector
 The Element Selector selects all HTML elements of a particular type

Example:
 div {
     background-color: red;
 }
 This selects all <div> elements

Example:
 <div>Hello</div>
 <div>Hello Again</div>

 Both <div> elements will get the same styling 

-> The drawback is that if there are multiple elements of the same type, the style is applied to all of them

Class Selector
 A Class Selector is Used to select elements having a particular class
 It is represented usinng: .

Example:
 HTML:
 <div class="red">
    Hello
    </div>

 .red {
    background-color: red;
 }

 Only elements with:
 class="red"
 will receive this style

Multiple elements can use the same class:
 <div class="red">One</div>
 <p class="red">Two</p>

Both can be styled using:
 .red{
   color: red;
 }

ID Selector
 An ID Selector selects an element using its id
 It is represented using:
 #
Example:
HTML:
 <div id="green">
      Hello
      </div>

CSS:
 #green {
     background-color: green;
 }

 The element with:
 id="green"
 will receive the style

 Class -> .
 ID    -> #

Example:
 .red { }
 #green { }

Child Selector 
 The Child Selector selects an element that is direct child of another element
 It uses:
 >

Example:
 div > p {
    color: blue;
 }

 This means:
   Select <p> elements that are direct children of <div>

Example:
 <div>
    <p>Hello</p>
  </div>

 Here <p> is a direct child of <div>, so the style applies

Important Difference
Consider:
 <div>
    <article>
       <p>Hello</p>
     </article>

  </div>

Here <p> is not a direct child of <div>

Therefore:
 div > p {
   color: blue;
 }
 will not select this <p>

Descendant Selector
 The Descendant Selector selects an element that exists anywhere inside another element
 It uses a space

Syntax:
 parent child {
    property: value;
 }

Example:
 div p {
    color: blue;
 }

 This selects <p> elements inside <div>, even if they are nested deeper 

Example:
 <div>
     <article>
         <p>Hello</p>
     </article>
 </div>

Here <p> is a descendant of <div>

Therefore:
 div p {
   color: blue;
 }
 will apply the style

Child vs Descendant Selector
 Child Selector
 div > p

Means:
 div
  ↓
  p
Only direct child

Descendant Selector
 div p
Means:
 div
  ↓ 
 article 
  ↓ 
  p

  Can select a child, grandchild, or deeper descendant

  >     -> Direct child
  space -> Any descendant

Universal Selector 
 The Universal Selector selects all HTML elements
 It is represented by:
 *
Example:
 * {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
 }
 This applies the style to all elements
 It is commonly used for basic CSS resets 

Pseudo-Class Selector
 A Pseudo-Class Selector selects an elementy based on a particular state or condition
 It uses:
 :
Example:
 a:hover {
    color: red;
 }
 When the mouse pointer moves over the link, the color changes to red

Common Pseudo-Classes
:hover
 Applies when the mouse is placed over an element
 a:hover {
    color: red;
 }

:visited
 Applies to a link that has already been visited
 a:visited {
   color: purple;
 }

:link 
 Selects an unvisited link
 a:link {
   color: blue;
 }

:actice 
 Applies while the link is being activated/clicked
 a:active {
     color: green;
 }

:first-child 
 Selects an element if it is first child of its parent
Example:
 p:first-child {
     color: red;
 }

CSS Box Model
 The CSS Box Model describes how every HTML element is represented as a rectangular Box
 It consists of four main parts:

        ┌─────────────────────────────┐
        │           Margin            │
        │  ┌───────────────────────┐  │
        │  │        Border         │  │
        │  │  ┌─────────────────┐  │  │
        │  │  │     Padding     │  │  │
        │  │  │  ┌───────────┐  │  │  │
        │  │  │  │  Content  │  │  │  │
        │  │  │  └───────────┘  │  │  │
        │  │  └─────────────────┘  │  │
        │  └───────────────────────┘  │
        └─────────────────────────────┘

The four parts are:
 Content
 Padding
 Border
 Margin

Content
 Content is the actual data inside an element
 It can be:
 Text
 Image
 Video
 Other HTML content
 The width and height  properties normally control the content area's dimensions
Example:
 .box {
    width: 300px;
    height: 100px;
 }

Padding
 Padding is the space between the content and the border
Example:
 .box {
   padding: 20 px;
 }

Meaning:
  Border
        ↓ 
 ┌─────────────────┐ 
 │ Padding         │ 
 │ ┌───────────┐   │ 
 │ │ Content   │   │ 
 │ └───────────┘   │ 
 └─────────────────┘

 Padding creates internal space inside the element 

Margin  
 Margin is the space outside the border
 It creates space between an element and other elements
Example:
  .box {
    margin: 20px;
  }

         Margin
            ↓ 
      ┌─────────────┐ 
      │    Border   │ 
      │   Content   │ 
      └─────────────┘ 
            ↑ 
        Outside 

 padding -> Space inside the border
 Margin -> Space outside the border

Border
 A border surrounds the content and padding

Example:
  .box {
     border: 2px solid red;
  }

 A border can have:
 Border width
 Border Style
 Border color

Separate properties
 .box {
     border-width: 2px;
     border-style: solid;
     border-color: red;
 }

Shorthand
 Instead of writing all three:
 border: 2px Solid red;

Border Radius
 border-radius is used to make the corners of a box rounded

Example:
 .box {
    border: 2px solid black;
    border-radius: 10px;
 }
 Large valuess create more rounded corners

Margin values
 Margin can be applied to all four sides
 .box {
   margin: 20px;
 }

 This applies:
 Top    -> 20px
 Right  -> 20px 
 Bottom -> 20px
 Left   -> 20px

Margin With Four values
 .box {
    margin: 10px 20px 30px 40px;
 }
 The order is:
 Top -> Right -> Bottom -> Left

Margin With Two values
 .box {
    margin: 10px 20px;
 }
 
Meaning:
 Top + Bottom -> 10px
 Left + Right -> 20px

Individual Margin properties
 You can set each side separately:
 .box {
    margin-top: 10px;
    margin-right: 20px;
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    margin-left: 40px;
 }

Padding Shorthand
 Padding works similarly to margin 

One value 
 padding: 20px;

All four sides:
 Top    -> 20px
 Right  -> 20px
 Bottom -> 20px
 Left   -> 20px

Two values
 Padding: 10px 20px;

 Top + Bottom -> 10px
 Left + right -> 20px

Four values
 Padding: 10px 20px 30px 40px;

Order:
 Top -> Right -> Bottom -> Left

Total width and Height
 The actual space occupied by an element can be larger than  its declared width and height

Total Width
 Total width =
 Content Width 
 + Left Padding 
 + Right Padding 
 + Left Border 
 + Right Border 
 + Left Margin 
 + Right Margin

Total Height
 Total Height =
 Content Height
 + Top Padding 
 + Bottom Padding 
 + Top Border 
 + Bottom Border 
 + Top Margin 
 + Bottom Margin

Example of Box Model Calculation
 Suppose:
    .box {
      width: 200px;
      padding: 15px;
      border: 10px solid red;
      margin: 5px;
    }

Total Width:
 200
 + 15 + 15
 + 10 + 10
 + 5 + 5
 = 260px

 So the total horizontal space is:
 260px

Margin collapse
 Margin Collapse occurs when vertical margins of certain-level elements overlap
 When two vertical margins collapse, the resulting margin is generally the larger of the two margins, rather than their sum 

Example:
 .box1 {
     margin-bottom: 30px;
 }

 .box2 {
     margin-top: 20px;
 }

You might expect:
 30px + 20px = 50px

 But when these vertical margins collapse:
 Maximum(30px, 20px)
 = 30px

 So the effective gap is generally:
 30px

Important Point About Margin Collapse
 Margin collapse mainly applies to vertical margins of block-level elements
 It does not mean that every pair of margins always collapses
 For example, horizontal margins do not collapse in the same Way

Important Difference
 Property	          Meaning
 content	          Actual content
 padding	          Space between content and border
 border	            Surrounds content and padding
 margin	            Space outside the border

Complete Example:
 <!DOCTYPE html>
 <html>
 <head>

 <style>

 .box
 {
   width: 20px;
   height: 100px;

   padding: 20px;

   border: 5px solid black;

   margin: 30px;

   border-radius: 10px;
 }
 </style>
 </head>
 <body>
 <div class="box">
     Hello World
 </div>

 </body>
 </html>

 Here:

 width         -> Content width
 Height        -> content height
 padding       -> space inside
 border        -> Boundary
 margin        -> Space outside
 border-radius -> Rounded corners

Font Family
 The font-family property is used to change the typeface of text

Example:
 h1 {
   font-family: Arial;
 }

You can provide multiple fonta as fallbacks:
 h1 {
   font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
 }
 If Arial is unavailable, the browser uses sans-serif

Common geneeric families:
 serif
 sans-serif
 monospace
 cursive
 fantasy

Font size
 The font-size property changes the size of text
Example:
 p {
  font-size: 20px;
 }

Commonly used units include:
 px
 em
 rem
 %
 For now, px is the simplest unit to understand 

Font Style
 The font-style property controls whether text is normal, italic, etc 

Example:
 p {
  font-style: italic;
 }

Common values:
 normal
 italic
 oblique

Font Weight
 The font-weight property controls the thickness of text
Example:
 p {
  font-weight: bold;
 }

Common values:
 normal
 bold
 lighter
 bolder

 Numeric values can also be used:
 p {
  font-weight: 700;
 }

Generally:
 400 -> normal
 700 -> bold 
 
Google Fonts
 Google Fonts can be used to add additional fonts to a website
 You can select a font from google Fonts and include it in your HTML/CSS
 One Common method is using a <link> in the <head>:

 <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Poppins&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">

 Then use it in CSS:
 body {
   font-family: "Poppins", sans-serif;
 }    

Another method is using @import inside CSS:
 @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Poppins&display=swap');)
Then:
 body {
   font-family: "Poppins", sans-serif;
 }

Line Height
 The Line-height property controls the vertical space between lines of text
Example:
 p {
     line-height: 1.5;
 }

It can also be given using a unit:
 p {
   line-height: 30px;
 }
 Increasing line-height can make patragraphs easier to read 

Letter spacing
 The letter-spacing property controls the space between individual characters
Example:
  h1 {
      letter-spacing: 2px;
  }

Negative values can also be used:
 h1 {
   letter-spacing: -1px;
 }

Text alignment
 The text-align property controls the horizontal alignment to text
Example:
 h1 {
   text-align: center;
 }

Common values:
 left
 right
 center
 justify

Example:
 p {
   tect-align:  justify;
 }

Text Transformation
 The text-transform property changes the capitalization of text
Uppercase
 p {
   text-transform: uppercase;
 }

Example:
 hello world

Becomes:
 HELLO World

Lowercase
 p {
   text-transform: lowercase;
 }

Capitalize
 p {
   text-transform: capitalize;
 } 
 The capitalizes the first letter of each word

Text Decoration
 The text-decoration property adds decorations to text
Example:
 p {
   text-decoration: underline;
 }

Common values:
 underline
 overline
 line-through
 none

Example:
 p {
   text-decoration: line-through;
 }

Text Decoration color
 The color of a text decoration can be changed separately

Example:
 p {
   text-decoration: underline;
   text-decoration-style: dotted;
 }

Common styles include:
 solid
 double
 dotted
 dashed
 wavy

Text Decoration thickness
 The thickness of the text decoration can be controlled using:
 text-decoration-thickness
Example:
 p {
   text-decoration: underline;
   text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
 }

Colors in CSS
 The color property changes the color of text

Example:
 p {
   color: red;
 }
 Background color can be changed using:
 background-color: yellow;

Color Keywords
 CSS provides predefined color name
Example:
 p {
   color: red;
 }

Other examples:
 blue
 green
 orange 
 purple 
 black 
 white 
 yellow
There are many predifined CSS color notation

Hexadecimal Colors
 Colors can be represented using hexadecimal notation
Example:
 p {
   color: #ff0000;
 }

Here:
 #ff0000 -> red

A common six-digit hex color follows:
 #RRGGBB
where:
 RR -> red
 GG -> green
 BB -> Blue 
 Each pair represents the intesity of that color component

Example:
 #000000 -> black
 #ffffff -> white
 #ff0000 -> red
 #00ff00 -> green
 #0000ff -> Blue

RGB Colors
RGB stands for:
 red
 green
 blue

Syntax:
 color: rgb(255, 0, 0);

 This represents red

Each component generally ranges from:
 0 -> 255

Example:
 color: rgb(0, 255, 0);
 Green

RGBA Colors
 RGBA adds an alpha channel to RGB
 R → Red
 G → Green
 B → Blue
 A → Alpha
Example:
 color: rgba(255, 0, 0, 0.5);
 Here the last value controls transparency

HSL Colors
 HSL stands for:
 H -> Hue
 S -> Saturation
 L -> Lightness

Syntax:
 color: hsl(0, 100%, 50%);
 HSL provides another way to describe colors

HSLA 
 HSLA adds an alpha value to HSL
 color: hsla(0, 100%, 50%, 0.5)
Here:
 H -> Hue
 S -> Saturation
 L -> Lightness
 A -> Alpha

Color Values Can Be Used in Other properties
 The same color formats can be used for properties such as:
 color
 background-color
 border-color
 text-decoration-color

Example:
 .box {
   color: white;
   background-color: #000000;
   border-color: red;
 }

 */

    













